WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:23.010
I'd like to welcome
everyone to this morning

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:26.160
to Yale's fourth
annual Day of Data.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.160
We're very happy to
have everyone here.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:30.540
My name is Jill Parchuck.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.790
I'm the Associate University
Librarian for Science,

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:34.200
Social Science, and Medicine.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.110
And I'll be your
host for the morning.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:38.970
Before we do anything,
I want to just give

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:40.920
a few little logistics.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:45.390
There will be photos and video
being taken throughout the day,

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:49.080
so that you're aware of that
and give a little courtesy

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:52.650
to the photographers
while things are going on.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:54.690
We ask you to silence
your cell phones.

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:56.130
I know everybody's
very important,

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:57.810
has a million things
going on, but we

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:00.990
can turn off the ringers.

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:03.930
Coffee you've already
seen, I hope, downstairs.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:07.200
Lunch will be in the same
location this afternoon.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.910
And we'll have some
instructions for you

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:12.600
at that point about how to
line up, queue up, to get down

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.990
to lunch so that we
don't have a mob scene.

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:16.980
Restrooms.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.110
There are two
restrooms right down

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.420
the corridor right
near where you came in,

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.307
and then there are a
few more down stairs.

00:01:23.307 --> 00:01:25.890
If you just go down the stairs,
and down the hallway, and down

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.170
another set of stairs.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.110
The schedule for the
day is on the web,

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:33.630
but we also had some we
handed out at the table

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:34.480
as you came in.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.534
So if you need a schedule,
you can pick one up there.

00:01:38.534 --> 00:01:40.200
First thing I'd like
to say is thank you

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.630
to all the sponsors
for the Day of Data.

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:44.540
First off the Office
of the Provost,

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:48.720
who has been generous and very
supportive in hosting this.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.670
The Center for Science
and Social Science

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:53.610
Information, many staff members
who helped to make the day

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:54.709
happen.

00:01:54.709 --> 00:01:56.250
The Center for
Teaching and Learning,

00:01:56.250 --> 00:02:00.240
who is supporting our
video set up for the day.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.580
The Digital Humanities
Lab, who also

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.860
heavily participated
in the planning.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:07.320
The Institution for
Social and Policy Studies,

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:09.990
who's been a great supporter of
Day of Data from the beginning

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:12.540
and did a lot of
the planning, also.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:15.540
The Yale Institution for
Network Science, also

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.940
big contributors to this event.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.850
And Sigma XI
Distinguished Visitor Fund

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:24.810
is also supportive, so we're
very grateful for our sponsors.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:28.050
A big thank you, also,
to the planning committee

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:31.440
for the Day of Data, which
has been superbly led

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:32.487
by Michelle Hudson.

00:02:32.487 --> 00:02:34.403
Michelle actually deserves
a hand of applause.

00:02:34.403 --> 00:02:37.301
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:42.060
Other members of that committee
have been Kayleigh 

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:46.350
Bohemier Cathy
DeRose, Erin Wachowicz

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:48.960
Melanie Maksin,
Themba Flowers, Paul

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.510
DiBello -- we couldn't have
done it without Paul--

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:54.480
Kate  Nyhan,
Peter Peduzzi, Limor Peer,

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:55.530
and George Stranz.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:59.350
So thank you to all of you.

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:01.420
One thing I think you
all might like to know

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:04.060
is that the Day of Data
also holds some follow up

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:06.040
events in the spring.

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:08.830
This year in line with
the Day of Data theme

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:12.760
of reproducibility, we'll be
having a spring reproducibility

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:16.720
workshop that will be conducted
by the Center for Open Science.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.940
And that'll be
held on March 3rd.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:21.280
So we're really looking
forward to that.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:24.550
There will also be a
series of other smaller

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:26.220
lectures in the spring.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:28.670
And they've been outstanding
in the past, as well.

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:31.300
So that information
will be sent out

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:34.930
to everybody who
registered for today.

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:37.390
Onto our outstanding
schedule of speakers.

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:39.520
Our first speaker
this morning is

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:43.990
Steve Girvin, Deputy Provost for
Research, and Eugene Higgins,

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:45.790
Professor of Physics.

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:47.920
Professor Girvin
joined the Yale faculty

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:51.730
in 2001 as a professor
of physics, and in 2007

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:54.550
was named Deputy
Provost for Research.

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:57.160
His portfolio includes
the Central Campus Science

00:03:57.160 --> 00:03:59.470
Departments, the School of
Forestry and Environmental

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:01.720
Studies, and the School
of Engineering and Applied

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:05.980
Science, as well as various
institutes and initiatives.

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:09.280
Additionally he collaborates
on science strategy

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:10.810
with the School of Medicine.

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:13.930
His academic research is in
the area of theoretical quantum

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:14.650
physics.

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:15.543
Steve, do you sleep?

00:04:15.543 --> 00:04:16.042
I mean--

00:04:16.042 --> 00:04:18.322
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:22.120
Professor Girvin holds
a bachelor's degree

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:24.010
from Bates College,
a master's degree

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:26.560
from the University of
Maine, and MS and Ph

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:28.150
degrees from Princeton.

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:31.660
In recognition of his research
and contributions in the field,

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:34.000
Professor Girvin has been
elected to the American

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.400
Association for the
Advancement of Science,

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:39.040
the American Academy
of Arts and Sciences,

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:41.740
the Royal Swedish
Academy of Sciences,

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:44.380
and the US National
Academy of Sciences.

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:47.560
In 2007 he was awarded
the Oliver E. Buckley

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:50.800
prize of the American
Physical Society.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:53.003
Without further
ado, Steve Girvin.

00:04:53.003 --> 00:04:55.961
[APPLAUSE]

00:05:17.653 --> 00:05:20.200
I'm hooked up to my--

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:21.890
one of my various science ties.

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:23.086
Today's astronomy.

00:05:23.086 --> 00:05:25.360
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:29.980
So and this-- is only going
to display on that screen.

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:32.050
Is that right?

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:34.630
So welcome.

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:38.680
And welcome to this
fabulous new space

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:42.550
that you'll be hearing more
about as the home of the Yale

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:44.260
Center for Research Computing.

00:05:56.212 --> 00:05:58.204
Is this you?

00:05:58.204 --> 00:05:59.200
Second one.

00:06:08.164 --> 00:06:11.152
[INAUDIBLE]

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:21.270
OK.

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:26.070
So I don't have to talk
to this crowd about the--

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:31.945
in this modern era, the flood
of data, the deluge of data,

00:06:31.945 --> 00:06:35.510
the tsunami of data which
is coming in to Yale

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:39.350
every day from all
parts of the world.

00:06:39.350 --> 00:06:41.610
You genomics people are
the worst offenders.

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:42.743
You know who you are.

00:06:42.743 --> 00:06:44.400
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:46.830
But here's another
source of data that

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:48.740
flows in to Yale every day.

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:50.070
Anybody recognize this?

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:52.080
This is the South Pole.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:55.210
We're standing 10,000
feet above sea level.

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:57.670
There's 10,000-- this
is snow on the surface.

00:06:57.670 --> 00:07:02.520
But below there is 10,000 feet
of solid ice, crystal clear.

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:05.580
Light can travel through
it huge distances.

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:09.840
And there are lots and lots of
photomultiplier detectors where

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:13.470
they melted a hole
thousands of feet down,

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:16.830
buried them in the ice,
and trillions and trillions

00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:21.060
of neutrinos from the
galaxy are passing

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:25.530
through every square centimeter
of that space every second.

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:28.680
And occasionally one of
them interacts with the ice

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.440
and gives off a bit of light.

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:35.040
And the entire
Antarctic ice shelf

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:40.620
is being used as a massive
detector, which is then

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:44.850
detecting these particles from
the Sun, and from the galaxy,

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:46.830
and from supernovas.

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:51.510
Amazing and weird source
of data which flows

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:53.760
into the university each day.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:59.370
And of course there are many,
many bigger sources than that.

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:04.560
We're-- cryo-electron microscopy
is about to start on the west

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:05.210
campus.

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:10.760
That will be attempting to catch
up with the genomics people,

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:16.020
but the genomics people will
probably still win that race.

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:18.230
So many of you--

00:08:18.230 --> 00:08:22.170
the locals will have
seen President Salovey's

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:25.860
communication to the
university 10 days ago

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:32.789
about the university priorities
and academic investments that

00:08:32.789 --> 00:08:36.570
will be made over the next
10 to 15 years at Yale.

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:38.980
And while there is
a bit of something

00:08:38.980 --> 00:08:43.470
in there for everybody, the far
and away the key top priority

00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:45.630
is going to be science.

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:49.680
It's the-- it's the
area that we need

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:54.930
to focus in to compete
on the world stage

00:08:54.930 --> 00:09:00.410
with the other really top
universities around the world.

00:09:00.410 --> 00:09:06.030
And so of course our
ability to handle data

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:09.730
and do great science is going
to be a key part of that.

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:12.000
And we have to work on
the infrastructure needed

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:13.360
to do that.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:20.010
You see here a rendering
of the new multi-hundred,

00:09:20.010 --> 00:09:24.890
million dollar science building
primarily devoted to biology.

00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:30.900
It's in place of
the Gibbs building,

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:33.820
which doesn't look
as nice as this.

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:35.070
[LAUGHS]

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:43.050
And the-- there will be space
for cryo-electron microscopy,

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:48.990
optical microscopy, all
kinds of biology labs,

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:50.820
physics and astronomy labs.

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:55.780
All of which are going to be
producing more and more data.

00:09:55.780 --> 00:10:00.520
So on data-- of course
ties in with Yale

00:10:00.520 --> 00:10:02.710
fulfilling its mission.

00:10:02.710 --> 00:10:05.980
We are an institution
that take our mission

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:10.450
to do good in the
world very seriously.

00:10:10.450 --> 00:10:12.670
We're trying to
improve the world

00:10:12.670 --> 00:10:19.330
through education, research,
practice, clinical practice,

00:10:19.330 --> 00:10:21.340
educate leaders.

00:10:21.340 --> 00:10:29.650
And free exchange of ideas is
absolutely a core principle.

00:10:29.650 --> 00:10:34.970
And there-- that is
now including data.

00:10:34.970 --> 00:10:43.360
So in exchange for
the opportunity

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:53.030
to do research and
fulfill Yale's mission,

00:10:53.030 --> 00:10:57.140
people have academic freedom
to choose the topics that they

00:10:57.140 --> 00:11:00.290
work on, but have
some responsibilities

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:04.250
to steward the scholarly
record, to provide access

00:11:04.250 --> 00:11:08.840
to the knowledge that
they're generating.

00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:13.070
And of course responsible
for making sure

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:15.860
that it's reproducible, and
for allowing other people

00:11:15.860 --> 00:11:19.440
to check the reproducibility
of that data.

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:26.120
And that's, in this day and
age, a super important topic.

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:28.100
It gets complicated
when you think

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:31.370
about the sort of traditional--
the tradition of keeping

00:11:31.370 --> 00:11:32.510
your raw data.

00:11:32.510 --> 00:11:34.550
I hate to keep picking
on the genomics people,

00:11:34.550 --> 00:11:38.700
but that's just becoming
a physical impossibility

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:39.650
in some cases.

00:11:39.650 --> 00:11:43.370
And you have to decide what
are the standards of the field

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:46.940
going to be in terms
of registering data,

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:48.680
and what fraction of the--

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:51.990
what version of the non-raw
data, et cetera, et cetera.

00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:55.250
So it's some
complicated questions

00:11:55.250 --> 00:11:59.710
as the ethos and different
subfields evolves.

00:11:59.710 --> 00:12:07.180
There are a lot of demands and
expectations from many sources.

00:12:07.180 --> 00:12:10.930
The government is
here to help us, and--

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:12.529
or was.

00:12:12.529 --> 00:12:15.367
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:22.030
There's been a-- there's been
a requirement for about nine

00:12:22.030 --> 00:12:27.730
years now that clinical trials
should be in-- the results--

00:12:27.730 --> 00:12:31.150
summaries of the results should
be available to the public.

00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:33.670
And they should be
registered before you start,

00:12:33.670 --> 00:12:36.250
and the results summarized
when you're done.

00:12:36.250 --> 00:12:41.530
That enforcement
has not been strong,

00:12:41.530 --> 00:12:45.230
but starting January 18th
it's going to be very strong.

00:12:45.230 --> 00:12:50.890
And we have to be prepared
and have systems in place

00:12:50.890 --> 00:12:55.300
to with the Institutional Review
Boards, et cetera, et cetera,

00:12:55.300 --> 00:12:57.550
to make sure that happens.

00:12:57.550 --> 00:13:01.300
Everybody knows that OSTP
has been promulgating

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:09.160
policies about open access to
data, so have the journals.

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:14.920
And there's various
reproducibility initiatives

00:13:14.920 --> 00:13:19.050
which are exposing potential
weaknesses in certain areas

00:13:19.050 --> 00:13:21.370
of certain fields of research.

00:13:21.370 --> 00:13:27.580
And I think it's very important
that we support those.

00:13:27.580 --> 00:13:31.960
And if we lose the
trust of the public

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:34.900
because our results
are not reproducible

00:13:34.900 --> 00:13:38.740
or appear to have been
influenced in some way

00:13:38.740 --> 00:13:42.740
inappropriately, it's
just all over for science.

00:13:42.740 --> 00:13:50.780
So we absolutely have to
support those initiatives.

00:13:50.780 --> 00:13:53.830
So we've been thinking
about this here,

00:13:53.830 --> 00:13:57.460
and I've been getting a lot
of help from Limor Peer,

00:13:57.460 --> 00:14:00.710
who's sitting over here, and
who helped organize this event.

00:14:00.710 --> 00:14:02.680
Thank you, Limor.

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:06.930
Thinking about policies
and guidelines, services

00:14:06.930 --> 00:14:11.530
and centers that Yale has
now, or should have now,

00:14:11.530 --> 00:14:14.440
and education and training--

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:20.430
not just for the young
people, but also for faculty

00:14:20.430 --> 00:14:25.820
who are beginning
to see the tsunami

00:14:25.820 --> 00:14:29.030
data coming towards them.

00:14:29.030 --> 00:14:33.590
So Limor has been chairing the
Research Data Policy Committee.

00:14:33.590 --> 00:14:38.600
And we've been trying to think
about as part of the, kind of,

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:42.860
infrastructure for supporting
data and data science at Yale

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:45.080
and sharing of data,
we need not just

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:48.620
physical infrastructure
but policies in place.

00:14:48.620 --> 00:14:52.850
And we're going to be,
in the coming months,

00:14:52.850 --> 00:14:56.000
on little roadshows
to various departments

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:57.830
and divisions around
the university talking

00:14:57.830 --> 00:15:01.760
about that work
that's been going on,

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:07.790
which includes some sort
of little white papers

00:15:07.790 --> 00:15:09.680
that we'll talk about.

00:15:09.680 --> 00:15:11.960
Principles that we
should keep in mind

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:14.690
as we develop further
policies as we build

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:18.350
our infrastructure,
and hardware,

00:15:18.350 --> 00:15:22.220
and consulting services,
and all those things.

00:15:22.220 --> 00:15:25.460
Also new at the university is
a Data Governance Committee,

00:15:25.460 --> 00:15:28.220
which is primarily
focused on Yale's

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:38.630
internal administrative data,
classroom occupancy, registrar

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:44.150
data, financial data, all
kinds of things like that.

00:15:44.150 --> 00:15:46.970
You know to clarify
who has access

00:15:46.970 --> 00:15:51.790
to what, how much
is public, just

00:15:51.790 --> 00:15:54.652
all kinds of policy questions.

00:15:54.652 --> 00:16:01.210
That and attempts to define
very precisely where is

00:16:01.210 --> 00:16:06.700
the primary record kept.

00:16:06.700 --> 00:16:11.020
And please don't keep
inaccurate copies of that.

00:16:11.020 --> 00:16:14.860
Keep a link to that
primary one and we'll

00:16:14.860 --> 00:16:17.305
be able to function more
efficiently, we hope.

00:16:17.305 --> 00:16:19.650
So it's primarily
about administration.

00:16:22.660 --> 00:16:25.890
We have a number of
services and centers

00:16:25.890 --> 00:16:29.610
already in existence-- the
Research Data Consultation

00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:30.930
Group.

00:16:30.930 --> 00:16:36.450
On this side of town there's
a stat lab in the CSSSI,

00:16:36.450 --> 00:16:41.070
that Jill can tell you about.

00:16:41.070 --> 00:16:43.684
In the Institute-- ISPS--

00:16:43.684 --> 00:16:47.610
ISPS, Institution for
Social and Policy Studies.

00:16:47.610 --> 00:16:50.860
This is the other
half of Limor's life.

00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:58.320
They're pretty
serious about keeping

00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:00.060
records that have been--

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:03.600
can still be accessed
and understood

00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:05.670
after you've written them.

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:08.920
You'll hear more, I think,
from Harlan Krumholz today.

00:17:08.920 --> 00:17:12.510
He's been involved with this
project in the med school.

00:17:12.510 --> 00:17:17.190
And there's-- for clinical
trials and big data things

00:17:17.190 --> 00:17:22.970
in the med school, there's
this data coordinating center.

00:17:22.970 --> 00:17:27.950
So there are many
places around campus

00:17:27.950 --> 00:17:31.250
where education and training
in data science are--

00:17:31.250 --> 00:17:34.800
exist or are coming
into existence.

00:17:34.800 --> 00:17:39.920
We have a 2 and 1/2
year old Yale Institute

00:17:39.920 --> 00:17:45.050
for Network Science,
which is bringing together

00:17:45.050 --> 00:17:48.380
people interested in
networks-- social networks,

00:17:48.380 --> 00:17:52.160
gene regulation networks,
ethernet networks,

00:17:52.160 --> 00:17:54.270
every possible thing
you can imagine.

00:17:54.270 --> 00:17:57.080
It's probably the most
heterogeneous group

00:17:57.080 --> 00:18:00.740
of people that are willing to
talk to each other on campus.

00:18:00.740 --> 00:18:03.350
And it's because they
can all come and learn,

00:18:03.350 --> 00:18:06.590
if they don't know it already,
the language of mathematics

00:18:06.590 --> 00:18:09.290
for describing networks.

00:18:09.290 --> 00:18:14.630
And of course big
data is part of that.

00:18:14.630 --> 00:18:19.400
It will-- assuming it is
approved by the corporation

00:18:19.400 --> 00:18:23.300
in this coming week--

00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:26.900
there will be a Data
Science Initiative

00:18:26.900 --> 00:18:31.030
announced involving--

00:18:31.030 --> 00:18:34.910
in the FAS involving the
Department of Statistics,

00:18:34.910 --> 00:18:38.630
Computer Science,
other mathematics

00:18:38.630 --> 00:18:42.530
departments on campus.

00:18:42.530 --> 00:18:45.920
And was recently
announced a Yale Center

00:18:45.920 --> 00:18:48.560
for Biomedical Data Science.

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:52.720
And one of my jobs is to
get these two coordinated

00:18:52.720 --> 00:18:55.350
and talking to each other.

00:18:55.350 --> 00:18:58.610
The Digital Humanities
Lab is another place

00:18:58.610 --> 00:19:06.830
where our humanist friends can
come and learn about numbers

00:19:06.830 --> 00:19:10.015
and other inhuman objects.

00:19:10.015 --> 00:19:13.610
The Stat Lab-- this Yale
Center for Research Computing

00:19:13.610 --> 00:19:18.520
you'll hear much more about
in the next talk from Karen.

00:19:18.520 --> 00:19:24.400
And there are a lot of data
hackathons going on, both from

00:19:24.400 --> 00:19:27.910
the-- organized from the
top down, and a lot from--

00:19:27.910 --> 00:19:30.310
like Hack Yale--
from the bottom up.

00:19:30.310 --> 00:19:35.290
Just students getting
together and having

00:19:35.290 --> 00:19:37.660
an event with
thousands of people

00:19:37.660 --> 00:19:42.400
from around the world showing
up to spend the weekend working

00:19:42.400 --> 00:19:45.020
on data.

00:19:45.020 --> 00:19:48.460
So this is intended
to not be readable.

00:19:48.460 --> 00:19:53.710
Just to illustrate the scale
of things that we either have

00:19:53.710 --> 00:19:56.560
or need to have in
the coming future

00:19:56.560 --> 00:20:01.300
in order to manage the
whole data lifecycle,

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:03.820
and for those parts of
it-- the research that

00:20:03.820 --> 00:20:05.290
are externally funded.

00:20:05.290 --> 00:20:09.640
They're kind of the pre-award,
award, and post-award parts.

00:20:09.640 --> 00:20:14.530
And some of these
things are in place.

00:20:14.530 --> 00:20:17.180
Many more need to be worked on.

00:20:17.180 --> 00:20:20.920
And so we have a-- we have
a big challenge ahead of us.

00:20:20.920 --> 00:20:26.470
And we'll be coming to
talk to the Yale folks

00:20:26.470 --> 00:20:29.500
in this room about
how we can make the--

00:20:29.500 --> 00:20:36.470
build the appropriate services,
infrastructure, policies,

00:20:36.470 --> 00:20:43.420
procedures to deal with
this flood of data,

00:20:43.420 --> 00:20:48.740
which is such an essential
part of research today.

00:20:48.740 --> 00:20:50.020
Thank you very much.

00:20:50.020 --> 00:20:53.370
[APPLAUSE]

